<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet component="quiz">
    <title>Learning Target 2,3,4 Quiz - MTH 204</title>

    <page>
        <p>
            This quiz covers course learning target #2, #3, and #4.
            Read all <term>DIRECTIONS</term> carefully.
            Good luck!
        </p>
        <!-- Learning Target 4 (Attempt 1) -->
        <exercise>
            <title>Learning Target 4, First Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can represent a linear system in three different ways and
                    be able to translate back and forth among the forms: as
                    <m>A\xvec=\bvec</m>, as a question about linear
                    combinations, and as a question about an augmented matrix.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Suppose the question is to find the solution set of the 
                    following linear system of equations.
                    <md>
                        \begin{alignedat}{4}
    4x \amp   \amp \amp {}+{} \amp z \amp {}={}  \amp 3 \\
    -x \amp  {}+{}  \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp z \amp {}={}  \amp 4 \\
    3x \amp  {}+{}  \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 4z \amp {}={}  \amp -3 \\
    -2x \amp  {}+{}  \amp 2y \amp \amp  \amp {}={}  \amp -2 \\
  \end{alignedat}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       How could you phrase the question about finding the solution set 
                       as a question
                        about
                     
                        <m>A\xvec=\bvec</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                            How could you phrase the question about finding the solution set 
                       as a question
                        about linear combinations?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What augmented matrix would you row reduce to find the solution set 
                        of the linear system?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <title>Learning Target 3, Second Attempt</title>

            <!-- Learning Target 3 (Attempt 2) -->
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    <em>   You can and should use Sage for this problem. You can find a page of
                    Sage cells at <url href="gvsu.edu/s/0Ng"/> (zero, capital N, lower case g). </em>
                    I can determine if and how a given vector can be written as
                    a linear combination of a given set of vectors and explain
                    what this means geometrically.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is the vector <m>\bvec = \threevec{11}{1}{2}</m> a
                        linear combination of <m>\uvec = \threevec{2}{1}{-1}</m>
                        and
                        <m>\wvec =
                        \threevec{1}{-1}{2}</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find the weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is the vector <m>\bvec = \threevec{11}{1}{3}</m> a
                        linear combination of <m>\uvec = \threevec{2}{1}{-1}</m>
                        and
                        <m>\wvec =
                        \threevec{1}{-1}{2}</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find the weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\uvec</m>, <m>\vvec</m>, and <m>\bvec</m> be
                        given by the figure below.
                        <!-- \scalebox{0.8}{\includegraphics{LT4-f-trans.eps}}  -->
                        Is <m>\bvec</m> a linear combination of <m>\uvec</m> and
                        <m>\vvec</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find the weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                        Write your response to the right of the figure.
                        <term>(Note</term>
                        <em> there is no scale on the axes, so you
                        can't determine coordinates for the various vectors;
                        just use the grid and figure appropriately.) </em>
                    </p>

                    <image source="images/LT3-grid21.png" width="25%"/>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <!-- Learning Target 2 (Attempt 3) -->
            <title>Learning Target 2, Third Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can use information about pivots to make conclusions about
                    the solutions of a system of linear equations, and explain
                    the underlying reasoning (using appropriate terminology)
                    regarding the existence and uniqueness of solutions and
                    consistency or inconsistency of a system.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For each of the following situations describing the pivots
                    in RREF of the matrix for a given linear system, determine,
                    with clear justification:
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                if the system is consistent or inconsistent, and
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                if the system is consistent, whether there are
                                infinitely many solutions or only one.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>

                <p>
                    For each situation, be sure to write a sentence to justify
                    your conclusions.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The coefficient matrix for a linear system with 3
                        equations and 3 unknowns has 3 pivots.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The augmented matrix for a linear system with 5
                        equations in 3 unknowns has 3 pivots, one in the
                        rightmost column.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The augmented matrix for a linear system with 2
                        equations in 4 unknowns has 2 pivots.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>